WEBVTT

1
00:00:58.976 --> 00:01:00.763
[Diane] <i>This is how it started.</i>

2
00:01:00.852 --> 00:01:03.094
<i>Four best friends and a book.</i>

3
00:01:03.730 --> 00:01:06.063
<i>Vivian was the one who had the idea.</i>

4
00:01:06.149 --> 00:01:07.765
<i>She'd seen it in a magazine,</i>

5
00:01:07.859 --> 00:01:09.691
<i>and it was that very first book</i>

6
00:01:09.778 --> 00:01:11.610
<i>that gave her a lifelong philosophy.</i>

7
00:01:12.698 --> 00:01:14.815
[man] You know what
I find sexiest about you?

8
00:01:15.617 --> 00:01:16.573
Do tell.

9
00:01:16.660 --> 00:01:18.026
You don't need me.

10
00:01:18.120 --> 00:01:19.531
[Vivian] I don't need anyone.

11
00:01:19.621 --> 00:01:22.455
It's the secret to my success.

12
00:01:22.541 --> 00:01:24.578
[Diane] <i>And successful she was.</i>

13
00:01:25.210 --> 00:01:27.577
<i>The hotel we were standing
in front of...</i> [scoffs]

14
00:01:27.671 --> 00:01:28.627
<i>...she owns.</i>

15
00:01:28.714 --> 00:01:29.955
<i>And ten years later,</i>

16
00:01:30.048 --> 00:01:32.256
<i>she tore it down
and built a new one.</i>

17
00:01:33.468 --> 00:01:35.380
[desk clerk]
Good morning, Miss O'Donnell.

18
00:01:35.470 --> 00:01:37.712
[chattering]

19
00:01:38.181 --> 00:01:39.342
Uh-huh.

20
00:01:40.601 --> 00:01:42.058
Yeah, I'll call you back.

21
00:01:42.144 --> 00:01:44.727
[men chattering]

22
00:01:46.398 --> 00:01:47.639
Slim?

23
00:01:47.733 --> 00:01:49.099
[Diane] <i>This is Arthur.</i>

24
00:01:49.651 --> 00:01:52.359
<i>The first time they had sex
was on a mix board.</i>

25
00:01:52.821 --> 00:01:54.312
<i>They were madly in love.</i>

26
00:01:54.406 --> 00:01:55.522
<i>He proposed.</i>

27
00:01:55.949 --> 00:01:57.315
<i>She said no.</i>

28
00:01:57.409 --> 00:01:58.490
<i>He left town.</i>

29
00:01:59.911 --> 00:02:01.743
[gasps] My, oh, my.

30
00:02:01.830 --> 00:02:02.866
Arthur Riley.

31
00:02:02.956 --> 00:02:04.868
What's it been, 40 years?

32
00:02:05.751 --> 00:02:08.459
That's impossible.
That would mean I was only six.

33
00:02:08.545 --> 00:02:10.411
- Still a wiseass, huh?
- [chuckles] Yeah.

34
00:02:11.006 --> 00:02:12.793
Old habits die hard.

35
00:02:13.216 --> 00:02:15.253
I got to get back
to this meeting, but, uh,

36
00:02:15.344 --> 00:02:16.960
are you going to be
in town for a while?

37
00:02:17.054 --> 00:02:19.296
I would hope so. I do live here.

38
00:02:19.389 --> 00:02:20.675
I'll find you.

39
00:02:21.224 --> 00:02:22.590
It's good to see you, Slim.

40
00:02:25.020 --> 00:02:26.977
[Diane] <i>Now, Sharon
was Vivian's roommate</i>

41
00:02:27.064 --> 00:02:28.600
<i>as an undergrad at Stanford.</i>

42
00:02:28.940 --> 00:02:30.772
<i>- Then she went to law school.</i>
- [gavel bangs]

43
00:02:31.151 --> 00:02:34.235
<i>Of the 301 people
in her graduating class,</i>

44
00:02:34.613 --> 00:02:35.945
<i>62 were women.</i>

45
00:02:36.031 --> 00:02:39.149
<i>Of the 62,
one became a federal judge.</i>

46
00:02:45.874 --> 00:02:47.331
He's on the phone now?

47
00:02:47.834 --> 00:02:49.245
Ladies and gentlemen,
we're going to take

48
00:02:49.336 --> 00:02:50.998
a five-minute recess.

49
00:02:51.088 --> 00:02:52.374
All rise.

50
00:02:53.465 --> 00:02:56.003
This court will stand
in recess for five minutes.

51
00:02:56.385 --> 00:02:59.878
No, no, no.
You're never interrupting.

52
00:02:59.971 --> 00:03:01.212
How is Hawaii?

53
00:03:02.015 --> 00:03:04.678
You got en... engaged?

54
00:03:04.768 --> 00:03:07.055
Why? I mean, wow!

55
00:03:07.145 --> 00:03:09.512
I didn't mean "why,"
I meant "wow." Wow.

56
00:03:10.857 --> 00:03:13.816
Well, if I sound judgmental, that...

57
00:03:13.902 --> 00:03:15.063
That's a professional hazard.

58
00:03:15.612 --> 00:03:16.978
Who's Cheryl?

59
00:03:19.241 --> 00:03:22.484
[inhales] Oh, I didn't know
your father was seeing someone.

60
00:03:22.577 --> 00:03:24.284
Good for him.

61
00:03:24.371 --> 00:03:26.237
And you. Good for both...

62
00:03:26.331 --> 00:03:28.618
Congratulations all around. Yeah.

63
00:03:28.709 --> 00:03:31.326
[Diane] <i>In 1985, Sharon married Tom</i>

64
00:03:31.420 --> 00:03:32.877
<i>because he made her laugh.</i>

65
00:03:32.963 --> 00:03:35.580
<i>By 1999, she was no longer laughing.</i>

66
00:03:35.674 --> 00:03:37.882
<i>And the final straw came
when Tom asked her</i>

67
00:03:37.968 --> 00:03:39.755
<i>what "Don Quix-ote" was about.</i>

68
00:03:40.303 --> 00:03:42.260
<i>The marriage ended
and Sharon bought a cat.</i>

69
00:03:42.347 --> 00:03:43.883
[cat meows]

70
00:03:43.974 --> 00:03:46.091
[Diane] <i>Carol put herself
through culinary school,</i>

71
00:03:46.184 --> 00:03:48.221
<i>working as a waitress at a diner.</i>

72
00:03:50.689 --> 00:03:52.681
<i>She had two goals in life:</i>

73
00:03:53.316 --> 00:03:56.809
<i>one, to own and operate
her own restaurant,</i>

74
00:03:56.903 --> 00:04:01.318
<i>and two, to marry the man
of her dreams, Bruce Colby.</i>

75
00:04:01.408 --> 00:04:03.024
<i>Since the moment they met,</i>

76
00:04:03.118 --> 00:04:05.826
<i>they've not been able to
keep their hands off each other.</i>

77
00:04:05.912 --> 00:04:08.154
- [squeals] Ooh!
- Happy anniversary.

78
00:04:08.248 --> 00:04:10.865
Happy anniversary.

79
00:04:12.753 --> 00:04:13.994
[Carol] Dance lessons!

80
00:04:14.087 --> 00:04:15.669
- Oh.
- Yep.

81
00:04:15.756 --> 00:04:17.122
- Wow.
- Yes.

82
00:04:17.215 --> 00:04:19.332
Because the No Kid Hungry fundraiser

83
00:04:19.426 --> 00:04:22.635
is a talent show this year
and I signed us up.

84
00:04:23.305 --> 00:04:25.137
- To be in the talent show?
- Yes!

85
00:04:25.223 --> 00:04:27.340
I thought it was something fun
we could do together

86
00:04:27.434 --> 00:04:28.800
now that you're retired.

87
00:04:28.894 --> 00:04:31.307
Now this is gorgeous!

88
00:04:33.648 --> 00:04:35.605
- Oh!
- Eargasms!

89
00:04:36.234 --> 00:04:37.725
Yeah, I got us both a pair.

90
00:04:38.403 --> 00:04:41.237
Multiple Eargasms.
Who does not love that?

91
00:04:41.323 --> 00:04:45.363
Well, no, it's so you can watch TV
in bed without disturbing me,

92
00:04:45.452 --> 00:04:47.694
and my snoring
won't keep you awake.

93
00:04:48.789 --> 00:04:50.451
- Huh?
- That's very thoughtful.

94
00:04:52.250 --> 00:04:54.913
What do you say we get home and...

95
00:04:55.003 --> 00:04:56.960
- I say yes.
- Yay.

96
00:04:57.047 --> 00:04:59.380
[Bruce snores]

97
00:04:59.925 --> 00:05:01.416
[snores louder]

98
00:05:02.219 --> 00:05:03.755
[Diane] <i>And then, there's me.</i>

99
00:05:03.845 --> 00:05:06.178
<i>I didn't know what my path
was going to be,</i>

100
00:05:06.264 --> 00:05:08.677
<i>and then it hit me,
with a wave of nausea.</i>

101
00:05:09.142 --> 00:05:11.600
<i>Because of this guy,
Harry Whittaker.</i>

102
00:05:11.978 --> 00:05:13.890
<i>We got married, and I became a...</i>

103
00:05:13.980 --> 00:05:15.437
- [women] Mom!
- [Diane] What?

104
00:05:15.524 --> 00:05:16.810
[woman] Stop already.

105
00:05:16.900 --> 00:05:20.234
What? No!
I just need to clear the caulk.

106
00:05:20.320 --> 00:05:22.812
- Clear the caulk?
- Yes.

107
00:05:22.906 --> 00:05:25.774
There's a cute little ladybug
trying to get out the window.

108
00:05:25.867 --> 00:05:28.450
Okay, someone's been watching
way too much HGTV.

109
00:05:28.537 --> 00:05:30.779
- Huh?
- Mom, put the screwdriver down.

110
00:05:31.289 --> 00:05:32.245
What?

111
00:05:32.332 --> 00:05:33.664
- Mom.
- Uh-huh?

112
00:05:33.750 --> 00:05:34.911
- We've been talking.
- Yes?

113
00:05:35.001 --> 00:05:36.993
We want to move you to Arizona.

114
00:05:37.087 --> 00:05:39.750
Move me? Oh, my God.
What am I, cattle?

115
00:05:39.840 --> 00:05:42.048
[Diane] <i>These are our two daughters.</i>

116
00:05:42.133 --> 00:05:46.093
<i>Harry is an accountant.
Well, he was an accountant.</i>

117
00:05:46.429 --> 00:05:48.421
Your house is falling apart,

118
00:05:48.515 --> 00:05:50.177
and you don't have Dad to fix it.

119
00:05:50.267 --> 00:05:52.509
I know, but, my friends,
they live here.

120
00:05:52.811 --> 00:05:55.474
And your family lives there.

121
00:05:55.564 --> 00:05:57.180
We just want you close to us, Mom.

122
00:05:57.274 --> 00:05:58.185
No, but...

123
00:05:58.275 --> 00:06:01.234
- Feel it out for the weekend.
- If not for you, for us, please.

124
00:06:01.319 --> 00:06:03.436
- It's what Dad would've wanted.
- All right.

125
00:06:03.530 --> 00:06:05.613
[Diane] <i>Well,
it's been over 40 years</i>

126
00:06:05.699 --> 00:06:09.534
<i>and through it all, every month,
we still get together for...</i>

127
00:06:10.537 --> 00:06:11.994
[wine cork pops]

128
00:06:12.080 --> 00:06:14.538
Wait. Wait, wait, wait.
Arthur as in Arthur?

129
00:06:14.624 --> 00:06:16.866
Wait a minute.
Are you talking about...

130
00:06:16.960 --> 00:06:19.919
- radio-station-sex Arthur?
- Yes!

131
00:06:20.005 --> 00:06:21.746
Proposed-with-a-milkshake Arthur.

132
00:06:21.840 --> 00:06:24.674
That Arthur.
And he's staying at the hotel.

133
00:06:24.759 --> 00:06:26.716
Does he not hate you, though?
He's got to.

134
00:06:26.803 --> 00:06:28.635
No. That was 40 years ago!

135
00:06:28.722 --> 00:06:31.009
Yeah, but still.
Oh! There she is.

136
00:06:33.226 --> 00:06:36.014
My son is engaged
and my husband is in Maui

137
00:06:36.104 --> 00:06:38.221
with a tartlet named Cheryl.

138
00:06:38.315 --> 00:06:39.180
[Diane] Oh...

139
00:06:39.274 --> 00:06:40.606
I need a drink.

140
00:06:40.692 --> 00:06:42.308
Your "husband"?

141
00:06:42.402 --> 00:06:45.145
You can't possibly still care
about what Tom is doing.

142
00:06:45.238 --> 00:06:46.604
I don't care.

143
00:06:46.698 --> 00:06:48.655
But the guy gets seasick
in a swimming pool.

144
00:06:48.742 --> 00:06:50.608
I mean, what the hell
is he doing in Maui?

145
00:06:51.119 --> 00:06:53.486
Sounds like he's
doing Cheryl in Maui.

146
00:06:53.580 --> 00:06:55.037
Oh, please.

147
00:06:55.332 --> 00:06:57.824
Who gets involved
in a relationship at 67?

148
00:06:57.918 --> 00:06:59.409
I mean, what is the point?

149
00:06:59.502 --> 00:07:02.415
The point is to get laid.
It's always the point.

150
00:07:02.881 --> 00:07:06.465
- Don't make me sick.
- Who still says "get laid"?

151
00:07:06.551 --> 00:07:08.008
Who still has any interest?

152
00:07:08.094 --> 00:07:10.962
No, no, no. I am not gonna
let us become those people.

153
00:07:11.056 --> 00:07:13.343
- What people...
- You know what people.

154
00:07:13.433 --> 00:07:16.016
The people who stop living
before they stop living.

155
00:07:16.102 --> 00:07:17.968
I haven't had sex since my divorce

156
00:07:18.063 --> 00:07:20.726
and it's been the happiest
18 years of my life.

157
00:07:21.316 --> 00:07:24.605
[gasps] That must be
some kind of record.

158
00:07:24.694 --> 00:07:28.108
I mean, what even happens
to a vagina after 18 years?

159
00:07:28.198 --> 00:07:30.656
I think Werner Herzog
did a documentary on that.

160
00:07:30.742 --> 00:07:33.450
Yeah. It's called
<i>The Cave of Forgotten Dreams.</i>

161
00:07:33.536 --> 00:07:35.869
Okay! Will you stop it?

162
00:07:35.956 --> 00:07:38.198
Moving on.
Let's talk about the book.

163
00:07:38.291 --> 00:07:40.533
Oh, God. The hiking book? Really?

164
00:07:40.627 --> 00:07:42.664
Come on! I liked it!

165
00:07:42.754 --> 00:07:46.168
It's such a remarkable undertaking.
Can you imagine?

166
00:07:46.257 --> 00:07:50.046
No, I cannot. I don't even
like walking to my mailbox.

167
00:07:50.136 --> 00:07:52.423
It's just an amazing story.

168
00:07:52.514 --> 00:07:55.222
So many layers. I wouldn't
even know how to break it down.

169
00:07:55.308 --> 00:07:56.674
I'll break it down for you.

170
00:07:56.768 --> 00:07:58.600
She hikes, she lost her boot,
she did heroin.

171
00:07:58.687 --> 00:08:00.178
Did you only read the back cover?

172
00:08:00.271 --> 00:08:01.512
[Vivian gulps] I wish.

173
00:08:01.606 --> 00:08:03.393
I kept wanting to shout at her,

174
00:08:03.483 --> 00:08:06.396
"Oh, wait ten years, honey.
Dry shampoo is coming."

175
00:08:06.486 --> 00:08:08.478
If you would ever
connect with something

176
00:08:08.571 --> 00:08:10.107
on a more emotional level...

177
00:08:10.198 --> 00:08:12.531
Emotional connection
is highly overrated.

178
00:08:12.617 --> 00:08:15.951
You have not had an emotional
connection for 40 years.

179
00:08:16.413 --> 00:08:18.575
Wow, that must be
some type of record.

180
00:08:18.665 --> 00:08:20.782
Yeah, but what happens
to emotions after 40 years?

181
00:08:20.875 --> 00:08:23.288
Okay, okay,
are you guys having fun?

182
00:08:23.378 --> 00:08:26.086
Oh, come on.
You know we love you.

183
00:08:26.172 --> 00:08:27.834
Maybe it's time
you did take a hike

184
00:08:27.924 --> 00:08:30.007
and try to reconnect
with your own true self.

185
00:08:30.093 --> 00:08:31.425
I'll buy you a backpack.

186
00:08:31.511 --> 00:08:34.219
I'll tell you how to reconnect
with your own true self,

187
00:08:34.305 --> 00:08:36.638
and it ain't by walking alone
through the desert.

188
00:08:37.392 --> 00:08:41.727
I would like to introduce you
to Christian Grey.

189
00:08:42.063 --> 00:08:43.099
Oh...

190
00:08:43.606 --> 00:08:44.687
Oh, no.

191
00:08:44.774 --> 00:08:46.766
[Vivian] It was a bestseller
made into a movie.

192
00:08:46.860 --> 00:08:49.022
- That is our theme this year.
- Oh, wow.

193
00:08:49.112 --> 00:08:50.853
We are not reading this.

194
00:08:50.947 --> 00:08:52.063
[Vivian] It's my month!

195
00:08:52.157 --> 00:08:53.944
When it's your month,
you can choose whatever

196
00:08:54.034 --> 00:08:55.616
boring, depressing book you want.

197
00:08:55.702 --> 00:08:58.194
I'm not sure
this qualifies as a book.

198
00:08:58.288 --> 00:09:00.120
Well, 50 million people
can't be wrong.

199
00:09:00.206 --> 00:09:03.074
To... To even be holding
this book is embarrassing.

200
00:09:03.168 --> 00:09:04.875
Who's judging you, your cat?

201
00:09:04.961 --> 00:09:07.419
I do like the idea of a romance.

202
00:09:07.505 --> 00:09:09.588
We are too old!

203
00:09:09.674 --> 00:09:12.417
[Carol] But it says right here
"for mature audiences."

204
00:09:12.510 --> 00:09:14.297
Yeah, that certainly sounds like us.

205
00:09:14.387 --> 00:09:18.381
We started this book club
to stimulate our minds.

206
00:09:18.475 --> 00:09:21.718
Well, from what I hear,
this book is quite stimulating.

207
00:09:21.811 --> 00:09:22.767
Oh, God.

208
00:09:22.854 --> 00:09:25.062
So... come on!

209
00:09:25.148 --> 00:09:27.515
- Let's toast to our new book.
- [Carol] All right.

210
00:09:27.609 --> 00:09:29.601
- Oh, good.
- Drink up. Hoist that glass.

211
00:09:29.694 --> 00:09:31.981
- [Carol] Yeah.
- Happy reading, ladies!

212
00:09:35.700 --> 00:09:36.736
[whispers] Oh...

213
00:09:37.619 --> 00:09:39.030
[grunts] Oh, God.

214
00:09:39.746 --> 00:09:41.112
[sighs] Okay.

215
00:09:49.005 --> 00:09:50.337
Oh, my... [groans]

216
00:09:50.423 --> 00:09:51.755
[groans] Oh, I didn't...

217
00:09:51.841 --> 00:09:53.833
I'm really so terribly...

218
00:09:54.177 --> 00:09:55.634
I'm so sorry.

219
00:09:56.596 --> 00:09:57.677
I... I...

220
00:09:57.764 --> 00:09:58.754
[Diane laughs softly]

221
00:09:58.848 --> 00:10:01.056
- You need some help?
- I'm sorry. No, I'm fine.

222
00:10:01.142 --> 00:10:03.429
I really didn't mean to disturb you.

223
00:10:03.520 --> 00:10:05.261
- Too late.
- I know, I know.

224
00:10:06.648 --> 00:10:08.310
Wow. [exhales]

225
00:10:08.399 --> 00:10:10.311
It's... This is so humiliating.

226
00:10:11.236 --> 00:10:12.477
[sighs]

227
00:10:12.570 --> 00:10:13.731
- Are you okay?
- I'm...

228
00:10:13.822 --> 00:10:15.984
- I'm really scared of flying.
- You shouldn't be.

229
00:10:16.908 --> 00:10:19.867
Oh, yeah, well,
I won't be once these...

230
00:10:20.703 --> 00:10:22.444
kick into my bloodstream.

231
00:10:22.539 --> 00:10:24.030
The safest way to travel.

232
00:10:24.124 --> 00:10:26.741
- Want to know why you think that?
- Why?

233
00:10:26.835 --> 00:10:30.078
Because you never met anybody
who's been in a plane crash.

234
00:10:30.171 --> 00:10:31.582
- And you want to know why?
- Why?

235
00:10:31.673 --> 00:10:33.209
Because they're all dead.

236
00:10:36.010 --> 00:10:38.047
I think those pills
are affecting you already.

237
00:10:38.138 --> 00:10:40.221
[chuckles] I think so.
Oh, my God.

238
00:10:40.306 --> 00:10:41.842
What... What takes you to Arizona?

239
00:10:41.933 --> 00:10:43.424
Oh, my...

240
00:10:43.893 --> 00:10:45.509
- My daughters live there.
- Hmm.

241
00:10:45.603 --> 00:10:47.060
Well, that should be fun.

242
00:10:47.147 --> 00:10:48.479
Not exactly.

243
00:10:49.023 --> 00:10:50.264
They're kind of forcing me to go.

244
00:10:50.358 --> 00:10:51.348
Why?

245
00:10:51.943 --> 00:10:53.525
[sighs] Well...

246
00:10:54.487 --> 00:10:58.071
my husband, he died last year, so...

247
00:10:58.992 --> 00:11:00.278
Plane crash?

248
00:11:00.368 --> 00:11:03.156
[laughs] Oh! You didn't say that.

249
00:11:03.830 --> 00:11:05.913
- Sorry, that was a bad joke.
- That's okay.

250
00:11:05.999 --> 00:11:08.582
- I'm really sorry for your loss.
- I'm sorry, too. No, well...

251
00:11:08.668 --> 00:11:10.955
Thank you, that's sweet.
Thank you so much.

252
00:11:12.172 --> 00:11:13.037
- Yeah...
- [thump]

253
00:11:13.131 --> 00:11:14.372
- Oh!
- [woman] Hey!

254
00:11:14.465 --> 00:11:15.455
[shrieks]

255
00:11:15.550 --> 00:11:16.711
[groans]

256
00:11:16.801 --> 00:11:18.667
Oh, I'm sorry. Oh, my God.

257
00:11:18.761 --> 00:11:20.798
[whispers] Oh, this is terrible.

258
00:11:21.472 --> 00:11:23.054
- Wow.
- I'm so...

259
00:11:23.141 --> 00:11:26.134
[stammers] That was the jet bridge
and we're still on the ground.

260
00:11:26.686 --> 00:11:27.972
- We're still on the ground?
- Yeah.

261
00:11:28.062 --> 00:11:31.226
I'm so terribly sorry.

262
00:11:32.442 --> 00:11:33.478
[groans]

263
00:11:33.818 --> 00:11:35.480
- I'm quite all right.
- Okay.

264
00:11:35.570 --> 00:11:37.983
Anything I can do to make
your flight more comfortable.

265
00:11:38.072 --> 00:11:40.189
I appreciate that,
thank you so much.

266
00:11:40.283 --> 00:11:42.445
[pop music playing]

267
00:11:42.535 --> 00:11:45.778
[inaudible]

268
00:11:46.748 --> 00:11:49.582
[whispers] Oh, Jesus.

269
00:11:52.170 --> 00:11:53.206
[sighs]

270
00:11:58.927 --> 00:12:00.589
Oh, wow.

271
00:12:07.143 --> 00:12:08.304
[sighs] Oh...

272
00:12:09.812 --> 00:12:10.848
Oh.

273
00:12:11.606 --> 00:12:12.687
Okay.

274
00:12:13.399 --> 00:12:15.516
[whispers]
Give me a break, okay?

275
00:12:16.361 --> 00:12:17.522
This...

276
00:12:17.612 --> 00:12:18.602
[scoffs]

277
00:12:18.696 --> 00:12:19.732
[Vivian] <i>Whoa!</i>

278
00:12:21.115 --> 00:12:23.323
[groans]

279
00:12:23.409 --> 00:12:25.867
[groans] Oh, my God, yes.

280
00:12:30.416 --> 00:12:32.032
- You know, I was wondering.
- Huh?

281
00:12:32.126 --> 00:12:34.083
That must have been one heck
of a book you were reading.

282
00:12:34.170 --> 00:12:35.377
May I ask what it was?

283
00:12:35.964 --> 00:12:38.081
<i>- Moby Dick.</i> That's what it was.
<i>- Moby Dick.</i>

284
00:12:38.174 --> 00:12:39.915
Uh-huh. Melville.

285
00:12:41.344 --> 00:12:43.757
I didn't realize Christian Grey
had a nickname.

286
00:12:46.015 --> 00:12:47.927
- Enjoy Arizona.
- Yeah, sure, of course.

287
00:12:48.017 --> 00:12:49.383
Not a problem.

288
00:12:58.403 --> 00:13:01.271
[assistant, over intercom] <i>Miss O'Donnell,
I have an Arthur Riley for you.</i>

289
00:13:02.407 --> 00:13:03.693
Is that so?

290
00:13:04.701 --> 00:13:06.158
Take a message.

291
00:13:06.703 --> 00:13:08.365
[Arthur] Take a message?

292
00:13:08.454 --> 00:13:09.615
Really?

293
00:13:09.706 --> 00:13:10.867
[sighs]

294
00:13:11.666 --> 00:13:13.248
I didn't realize that...

295
00:13:13.334 --> 00:13:14.620
[clears throat]
that you were here.

296
00:13:14.711 --> 00:13:17.454
We just saw each other
in the lobby two days ago.

297
00:13:18.339 --> 00:13:21.423
Pity. They say the memory
is the second thing to go.

298
00:13:23.761 --> 00:13:26.378
- What's the first thing to go?
- Can't remember.

299
00:13:28.141 --> 00:13:29.382
I'm impressed.

300
00:13:29.851 --> 00:13:31.513
- How did you find me?
- Google.

301
00:13:31.602 --> 00:13:34.390
- Google?
- Yeah. It's a service on the computer.

302
00:13:34.480 --> 00:13:35.766
Yeah, I know Google.

303
00:13:35.857 --> 00:13:38.770
Oh, well, I'm younger than you,
so I didn't know...

304
00:13:38.860 --> 00:13:41.318
Oh, cute. Cute.

305
00:13:41.404 --> 00:13:42.861
What do you say?

306
00:13:42.947 --> 00:13:44.813
May a gentleman buy you
a cup of coffee?

307
00:13:44.907 --> 00:13:47.024
Well, a gentleman certainly may.

308
00:13:47.410 --> 00:13:48.526
Do you know of one?

309
00:13:50.913 --> 00:13:52.745
It was the mid-'80s.

310
00:13:52.832 --> 00:13:55.996
I became obsessed
with risk management.

311
00:13:56.085 --> 00:13:57.917
You obsessed with work?

312
00:13:58.004 --> 00:13:59.040
That's crazy.

313
00:13:59.130 --> 00:14:01.622
I know, right? Shocking!

314
00:14:01.716 --> 00:14:06.177
I could see the system
was set up for trouble.

315
00:14:06.262 --> 00:14:08.424
Of course, none of the men
in my firm believed me.

316
00:14:08.514 --> 00:14:11.928
Well, men can be
a little slow on the uptake.

317
00:14:12.393 --> 00:14:14.305
Bad for them, good for me.

318
00:14:14.896 --> 00:14:16.012
And, um...

319
00:14:16.564 --> 00:14:18.851
you never picked up
a husband along the way?

320
00:14:20.902 --> 00:14:21.983
No!

321
00:14:22.987 --> 00:14:25.445
Like I said, I'm way too good...

322
00:14:25.907 --> 00:14:27.569
at forecasting risk.

323
00:14:27.658 --> 00:14:29.194
I figure by now, I've saved myself

324
00:14:29.285 --> 00:14:31.527
- a lot of money in legal fees.
- True dat.

325
00:14:33.164 --> 00:14:35.406
How about you?
Is there a Mrs. Riley?

326
00:14:35.500 --> 00:14:37.116
Oh, yes.

327
00:14:37.418 --> 00:14:40.161
The good Mrs. Riley
is back on the East Coast,

328
00:14:40.254 --> 00:14:43.747
having absconded with
my last name, my dog,

329
00:14:43.841 --> 00:14:45.673
and my co-op on the park.

330
00:14:46.344 --> 00:14:47.425
What did you do wrong?

331
00:14:47.512 --> 00:14:48.878
Well, let's just say

332
00:14:48.971 --> 00:14:52.214
I was a little less good
at assessing risk.

333
00:15:04.445 --> 00:15:07.483
Whoa! Tom, talk about
false advertising.

334
00:15:07.573 --> 00:15:09.735
[trumpet sounds through speakers]

335
00:15:16.749 --> 00:15:17.785
[whispers] Whoa!

336
00:15:22.171 --> 00:15:23.207
Jesus.

337
00:15:23.297 --> 00:15:25.084
[woman, over computer] <i>Join Tom
and Cheryl and millions of others</i>

338
00:15:25.174 --> 00:15:26.460
<i>who found love on Bumble.</i>

339
00:15:26.551 --> 00:15:31.012
Join Tom and Cheryl as they
copulate in a coconut tree.

340
00:15:31.097 --> 00:15:32.463
- [assistant] Judge Meyers?
- Yes.

341
00:15:32.557 --> 00:15:34.970
Hi. It's 12:15.

342
00:15:35.601 --> 00:15:38.935
Well, I really have
to stay with this, so, um...

343
00:15:39.730 --> 00:15:42.814
If you could just tell them that we have
to move it to next week, please.

344
00:15:42.900 --> 00:15:43.936
Of course. Yeah.

345
00:15:44.026 --> 00:15:45.562
[woman, over computer]
<i>Rev up your love life</i>

346
00:15:45.653 --> 00:15:47.019
<i>with these tips from Bumble...</i>

347
00:15:47.113 --> 00:15:49.605
<i>Make love happen now
and change your life...</i>

348
00:15:49.699 --> 00:15:51.190
Do you need anything else,
Your Honor?

349
00:15:51.284 --> 00:15:53.742
[woman, over computer] <i>A man
of your dreams is just a click away.</i>

350
00:15:53.828 --> 00:15:54.989
No, I don't. I'm fine.

351
00:15:55.079 --> 00:15:56.570
- Thank you.
- Yep.

352
00:15:57.331 --> 00:15:58.993
[door opens, closes]

353
00:15:59.584 --> 00:16:01.667
[Arthur] Here you go.
Make a wish.

354
00:16:05.381 --> 00:16:06.917
What are you doing?

355
00:16:07.008 --> 00:16:08.044
I made a wish.

356
00:16:08.134 --> 00:16:09.170
What did you wish for?

357
00:16:09.260 --> 00:16:11.843
I always wish for the same thing:
a healthy planet.

358
00:16:12.221 --> 00:16:13.757
That's not your wish.

359
00:16:14.474 --> 00:16:15.931
That's not even a wish!

360
00:16:16.017 --> 00:16:18.509
Wishes have to be personal,
not global.

361
00:16:18.603 --> 00:16:20.595
- Since when?
- Since forever.

362
00:16:21.397 --> 00:16:22.513
[sighs]

363
00:16:22.607 --> 00:16:25.771
And secondly, you can't
tell anybody your wish.

364
00:16:25.860 --> 00:16:27.772
Otherwise it negates the whole thing.

365
00:16:28.154 --> 00:16:30.692
No, God, you're not gonna
go in there. Don't do that.

366
00:16:30.781 --> 00:16:32.147
Well, somebody has to.

367
00:16:32.492 --> 00:16:33.858
You made an improper wish

368
00:16:33.951 --> 00:16:35.863
and you've jeopardized
the entire planet.

369
00:16:36.496 --> 00:16:38.112
Oh, God, you are insane.

370
00:16:38.206 --> 00:16:40.243
You are insane! Don't go in!

371
00:16:40.333 --> 00:16:41.665
[grunts] Oh...

372
00:16:41.751 --> 00:16:42.958
I have no choice here.

373
00:16:43.044 --> 00:16:44.660
You're obviously the type of person

374
00:16:44.754 --> 00:16:47.212
who's too delicate
to go in after her own penny.

375
00:16:47.590 --> 00:16:48.876
Oh, you think I'm delicate?

376
00:16:50.259 --> 00:16:51.921
[shrieks]

377
00:16:52.011 --> 00:16:53.547
See? Delicate.

378
00:16:53.638 --> 00:16:56.130
You know, what you're reading
as delicate is this.

379
00:16:56.224 --> 00:16:59.137
It's this bracelet
that costs as much as a car!

380
00:16:59.227 --> 00:17:00.593
Let's see that bracelet.

381
00:17:00.686 --> 00:17:02.143
- Oh, no. Not my phone...
- Oh, dear!

382
00:17:02.230 --> 00:17:03.892
- Oh, dear.
- No, no. Oh, boy.

383
00:17:03.981 --> 00:17:06.689
- Now you have done it.
- Oh, what do you mean?

384
00:17:06.776 --> 00:17:09.519
God, it's scary how addicted
you kids are to your phone.

385
00:17:09.612 --> 00:17:10.898
- [grunts]
- No, give it to me!

386
00:17:10.988 --> 00:17:12.775
Hold on one second here!

387
00:17:12.865 --> 00:17:14.982
[chuckles] I've never seen...

388
00:17:15.076 --> 00:17:18.160
You know, I think you might need
to talk to someone about this.

389
00:17:18.246 --> 00:17:20.579
I see why you lost
your co-op on the park.

390
00:17:20.665 --> 00:17:23.954
- I hope that's waterproof.
- Oh, no. [squeals]

391
00:17:25.336 --> 00:17:26.292
[chuckles]

392
00:17:26.379 --> 00:17:27.961
[whistle blowing]

393
00:17:30.174 --> 00:17:32.382
- No bathing in the fountain.
- [Arthur] Okay!

394
00:17:32.468 --> 00:17:34.209
But technically, we're not bathing.

395
00:17:34.303 --> 00:17:36.966
- Arthur.
- And she started it.

396
00:17:37.056 --> 00:17:39.719
He doesn't care who started it!
It was him.

397
00:17:39.976 --> 00:17:41.467
- Out.
- All right.

398
00:17:41.561 --> 00:17:43.928
Oh! Ah, wait a minute.

399
00:17:45.273 --> 00:17:48.107
- I found it.
- Oh, that's so great.

400
00:17:48.192 --> 00:17:50.434
Oh! Did you see that?

401
00:17:50.861 --> 00:17:53.604
Would you mind just taking a photo?

402
00:17:55.449 --> 00:17:57.156
[sighs] I hate my life.

403
00:18:01.205 --> 00:18:02.946
It might take a little bit of time,

404
00:18:03.040 --> 00:18:04.952
but you're gonna love it
here in Arizona.

405
00:18:05.042 --> 00:18:06.578
- I don't...
- Let's face it...

406
00:18:06.669 --> 00:18:08.831
You being here
makes more sense for all of us.

407
00:18:08.921 --> 00:18:11.834
We can't jump on a plane
every time something happens to you.

408
00:18:11.924 --> 00:18:13.631
What are you talking about,
"happens to me"?

409
00:18:13.718 --> 00:18:15.505
You could slip and fall
at any moment.

410
00:18:15.595 --> 00:18:17.632
I don't want to overstate the dangers

411
00:18:17.722 --> 00:18:19.930
for a woman your age living alone,

412
00:18:20.016 --> 00:18:22.599
but I did research online,
and it's pretty alarming.

413
00:18:22.685 --> 00:18:25.177
But you do know, I mean,
I'm really not that old.

414
00:18:25.271 --> 00:18:27.934
Well, no, you're not, Mom,
but neither was Dad.

415
00:18:28.316 --> 00:18:29.807
I know, honey, I know.

416
00:18:29.900 --> 00:18:30.981
I need a coffee.

417
00:18:31.068 --> 00:18:33.276
Well, the good coffee
is all the way upstairs.

418
00:18:33.362 --> 00:18:34.898
Mom, why don't you wait down here?

419
00:18:34.989 --> 00:18:36.150
Wait down here? Sure.

420
00:18:36.240 --> 00:18:38.607
God forbid, I can't go up
an escalator at my age.

421
00:18:38.701 --> 00:18:41.159
But hurry back. I could slip and fall
at any moment.

422
00:18:41.245 --> 00:18:42.861
- Just sit there, Mommy.
- Ooh!

423
00:18:42.955 --> 00:18:45.823
- I'll get you a chamomile?
- [sighs] Oh, God. Yeah, thanks.

424
00:18:45.916 --> 00:18:47.748
- Be careful!
- Uh-huh.

425
00:18:51.464 --> 00:18:53.376
[old man] They brought
the kids to see you?

426
00:18:53.466 --> 00:18:55.002
- Yeah.
- [old man] That's good.

427
00:18:55.092 --> 00:18:57.459
- Oh, God, what?
- [cell phone ringing]

428
00:18:59.889 --> 00:19:01.676
- Hello.
- [Carol] <i>Have you ever been spanked?</i>

429
00:19:01.766 --> 00:19:04.053
- What?
- As an adult, sexually.

430
00:19:04.143 --> 00:19:07.136
I mean, is this
what people are doing?

431
00:19:07.229 --> 00:19:09.642
Carol, it's Diane.
You dialed the wrong number.

432
00:19:09.732 --> 00:19:11.598
No. Have you not read the book?

433
00:19:11.692 --> 00:19:13.399
[Diane] <i>Yes. I've read it.
Of course I have.</i>

434
00:19:13.486 --> 00:19:15.819
- And?
<i>- I don't know who her lawyer is,</i>

435
00:19:15.905 --> 00:19:17.817
<i>but she should not have
signed that contract.</i>

436
00:19:17.907 --> 00:19:19.694
Look, clearly,
we have things to discuss.

437
00:19:19.784 --> 00:19:21.901
I'm gonna call
an emergency book club.

438
00:19:21.994 --> 00:19:24.202
That means you got to
get back here, pronto.

439
00:19:24.288 --> 00:19:26.450
I'd come back
for an emergency colonoscopy,

440
00:19:26.540 --> 00:19:28.907
but it's just a little bit
more complicated here.

441
00:19:29.001 --> 00:19:31.744
Oh, God. How is life in Scottsdale?

442
00:19:32.171 --> 00:19:34.879
Well, I don't know, I feel like I'm...

443
00:19:34.965 --> 00:19:38.333
- [snoring]
- ...in an episode of <i>The Walking Dead.</i>

444
00:19:38.427 --> 00:19:41.010
Wait a second. You sound
out of breath. Where are you?

445
00:19:41.097 --> 00:19:44.556
I'm going to a...
a dance rehearsal and I'm late.

446
00:19:44.642 --> 00:19:46.725
- You're late? You're never late.
- I know!

447
00:19:46.811 --> 00:19:49.394
This book has got me
in a total tizzy. Just get back here.

448
00:19:49.480 --> 00:19:51.517
I'll get back there. Bye.

449
00:19:53.442 --> 00:19:54.853
[sighs]

450
00:19:55.319 --> 00:19:56.355
- [Carol] Wow.
- Chin up.

451
00:19:56.445 --> 00:19:57.561
[Latin music playing]

452
00:19:57.655 --> 00:20:01.865
And one, two, three.
Go, two, three.

453
00:20:01.951 --> 00:20:05.115
- One, two, three.
- One, two, three. Wait.

454
00:20:05.204 --> 00:20:06.615
- Stop!
- [music stops]

455
00:20:07.206 --> 00:20:09.823
Stop, stop, stop!

456
00:20:10.292 --> 00:20:12.830
Honey, I think we're supposed
to go this way.

457
00:20:12.920 --> 00:20:13.910
To the left.

458
00:20:14.004 --> 00:20:15.711
Right. Which is what I did.

459
00:20:15.798 --> 00:20:18.040
His left. This way.

460
00:20:18.676 --> 00:20:21.510
Back, two, three.
Side, two, three.

461
00:20:21.595 --> 00:20:24.087
Okay, I got it. I was wrong.

462
00:20:24.181 --> 00:20:25.843
I was wrong, you were right.

463
00:20:25.933 --> 00:20:27.469
My little Fred Astaire.

464
00:20:27.560 --> 00:20:29.426
I will follow you
wherever you want to go.

465
00:20:29.520 --> 00:20:31.603
- How about lunch?
- We start again.

466
00:20:31.689 --> 00:20:33.476
You know what? I'm wondering...

467
00:20:33.566 --> 00:20:35.023
Do you think it would be
possible for us

468
00:20:35.109 --> 00:20:37.567
to just skip straight
to our choreography?

469
00:20:38.404 --> 00:20:41.568
We must first learn... to dance!

470
00:20:42.366 --> 00:20:43.902
Right. I know.

471
00:20:43.993 --> 00:20:46.906
But I sort of do know
how to dance. Come on, I do.

472
00:20:46.996 --> 00:20:49.784
I still remember
the whole tap dance

473
00:20:49.874 --> 00:20:51.661
to "Red, Red Robin Goes
Bob, Bob, Bobbin' Along"

474
00:20:51.751 --> 00:20:53.367
that I did when I was six.

475
00:20:53.461 --> 00:20:55.248
It's like savant-ish.

476
00:20:55.337 --> 00:20:57.920
[in Hungarian]
Sounds like you peaked early.

477
00:20:58.007 --> 00:20:59.293
What?

478
00:20:59.383 --> 00:21:01.249
- What did she say?
- You're asking me?

479
00:21:01.761 --> 00:21:06.552
So, what is going on
with little Ginsburg here?

480
00:21:07.475 --> 00:21:10.684
Well, she's not herself lately.

481
00:21:10.770 --> 00:21:12.853
She... She has no energy.

482
00:21:12.938 --> 00:21:16.147
She's really lost the spark of life.

483
00:21:16.233 --> 00:21:18.099
She's just kind of blah.

484
00:21:18.194 --> 00:21:20.686
It sounds like we have
a lethargic pussy on our hands.

485
00:21:21.113 --> 00:21:22.229
Seriously?

486
00:21:22.865 --> 00:21:24.322
Have there been
any changes at home?

487
00:21:24.408 --> 00:21:26.775
Any new people or pets
moving in or out?

488
00:21:26.869 --> 00:21:28.451
[chuckles] At home? Oh, God, no.

489
00:21:28.537 --> 00:21:30.870
There hasn't been
a change at home forever.

490
00:21:30.956 --> 00:21:32.367
And what about diet?

491
00:21:33.000 --> 00:21:36.038
I've tried a few, but I like to eat.

492
00:21:36.128 --> 00:21:39.667
So whatever weight I take off,
I put right back on, so...

493
